values
  ('capability:security_audit_pass', 'Security advisor audit (section 25)', 'Ran Supabase''s real security advisors against production. capability_registry itself confirmed correctly covered by its own service-role-only RLS policy (not in the "RLS enabled, no policy" list -- that pattern is itself deny-by-default and intentional for ~35 other platform-internal tables, not a gap). Nothing built this session introduced a new finding. Two real, pre-existing, unaddressed items surfaced, deliberately NOT touched blind: (1) three SECURITY DEFINER functions (check_and_increment_audit_rate_limit, claim_social_agent_action, claim_social_agent_action_tenant) callable by anon/authenticated -- plausibly intentional but not traced deeply enough this pass to confirm, so left alone rather than risk breaking a real, working system; (2) Leaked Password Protection is disabled in Supabase Auth -- a real, safe, one-click dashboard setting with no legitimate downside, but no available MCP tool can toggle Auth service config (only SQL/schema-level tools exist).', 'ecosystem', null, null, null, 'Engineering', null, null, 'read', 'platform_only', 'free', 'read', 'mcp__claude_ai_Supabase__get_advisors (security), run live against production 2026-09-02', 'PARTIAL', 'Audit run and findings recorded honestly; the two real pre-existing gaps are not yet remediated -- (1) needs deeper tracing before touching, (2) needs the owner to toggle in the Supabase dashboard (Authentication -> Policies -> Leaked Password Protection) since no available tool can do it.', 'Leaked Password Protection toggle requires Supabase dashboard access (Auth service config, not exposed via available SQL/schema tools).', now(), 'claude_session_2026-09-02')
